  "updated": "2026-10-20T23:55:27.433310+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..A very active weather pattern is expected across the \nstate through the next week. A strong low pressure system just off \nthe California coast today, will move inland and through the Desert \nSouthwest through Thursday. This storm is unusually strong for this \ntime of year and with that will come a variety of hazards. The low \neventually lifts through the Great Basin on Thursday, dragging a \ncold front through the region late Thursday into Friday. A piece of \nenergy breaks away from the departing low, then becomes a closed low \nover Arizona and lingers through the weekend. \n\nLate Tonight though Thursday...A very active couple of days are \nexpected for Wednesday and Thursday across the entire forecast area. \nLet's break it down by hazard. \n\nRainfall: Strong moisture advection from the south will continue \nthrough Thursday. There is a good plume of moisture being brought up \nfrom the western coast of Mexico and through the Gulf of California. \nSome of this moisture is left over from post-tropical cyclone Trudy \nand is enhanced by the continued active tropical activity in the \neastern Pacific. Showers and thunderstorms will develop by late \ntonight and spread northward through the state and become widespread \nin coverage on Wednesday and Thursday. PWAT values have risen \nslightly over the past several model runs and range from 0.8-1.25\", \nwhich are anomalously high and above the 95 percentile for the \nregion this time of year. Rainfall amounts will vary across the \nregion but expect widespread 1.0-3.0\" amounts to occur. Some locally \nhigher amounts between 3-5\" are certainly possible, especially in \nareas that see continued convective storms and multiple rounds of \nprecipitation. Models continue to show the highest amounts along and \nsouth of the Mogollon Rim. Additionally, areas across the Navajo \nNation may see 1-2\" of rainfall.\n\nFlooding/Flash Flooding: The expected rainfall combined with the \nfact that we have wet antecedent conditions, will result in a flood \nand flash flood risk. The convective nature of storms over the next \ntwo days could result fairly high precipitation rates, which may \nlead to flash flooding. Be advised that area creeks and streams \nalong with main stem rivers may see 2-3 foot rises. The Flood Watch \nis in effect for the entire forecast area from early Wednesday \nmorning through late Tuesday evening. \n\nSevere Weather: A strong mid-level jet is expected to move over the \nregion through the next couple of days. Strong southwesterly winds \nwill strengthen and combine with increasing moisture through the mid \nlevels, with steepening lapse rates. Shear is high with this system \n(50+ kts) and CAPE values are higher than usual for mid/late October \nand in the 500-1000 J/kg range. A limiting factor for stronger \nstorms would be the increasing cloud cover but we look to have \nenough shear and lingering instability to keep storms propagating \nthrough the evening and overnight periods. Storm relative helicity \nremains high with modeled helicity updrafts showing support for \nsupercell activity and rotating storms. The Storm Prediction Center \nhas placed parts of our area in an Enhanced Risk (category 3 out of \n5) of severe weather for Wednesday and in a Slight Risk (category 2 \nout of 5) for the much of eastern Arizona for Thursday. All severe \nhazards (damaging winds, large hail, a few tornadoes). \n\nBe prepared and have a way to receive warnings!\n\nFriday and onward...The main low pulls off to the east but some of \nits energy separates from the flow and may become a closed low over \nArizona through the weekend and into early next week. For now, \nshower and thunderstorm activity decreases by late Thursday into \nearly Friday with only isolated coverage for Friday through the \nweekend. Cooler temperatures are expected to continue with generally \nlight winds.",
